        </PAGEOBJECT>
        <PAGEOBJECT XPOS="-321.991514285714" YPOS="937.5968" OwnPage="-1" ItemID="239347920" PTYPE="4" WIDTH="331.615714285714" HEIGHT="287.9621" FRTYPE="0" CLIPEDIT="0" PWIDTH="1" PLINEART="8" LOCALSCX="1" LOCALSCY="1" LOCALX="0" LOCALY="0" LOCALROT="0" PICART="1" SCALETYPE="1" RATIO="1" COLUMNS="2" COLGAP="7.2" AUTOTEXT="0" EXTRA="0" TEXTRA="0" BEXTRA="0" REXTRA="0" VAlign="0" FLOP="1" PLTSHOW="0" BASEOF="0" textPathType="0" textPathFlipped="0" path="M0 0 L331.616 0 L331.616 287.962 L0 287.962 L0 0 Z" copath="M0 0 L331.616 0 L331.616 287.962 L0 287.962 L0 0 Z" gXpos="191.194085714286" gYpos="1494.14399999999" gWidth="0" gHeight="0" PSTYLE="Default Paragraph Style" LAYER="0" NEXTITEM="-1" BACKITEM="-1">
            <StoryText>
                <DefaultStyle PARENT="Default Paragraph Style" LINESP="10" FONT="Bell MT Regular" FONTSIZE="9" KERN="0" LANGUAGE="en_US"/>
                <ITEXT FONT="Bell MT Bold" FONTSIZE="9" CH="Elliot Rice"/>
                <para PARENT="Default Paragraph Style" ALIGN="1" LINESP="10"/>
                <ITEXT FONT="Bell MT Italic" FONTSIZE="9" KERN="0" CH="Puts the Q in QPA. "/>
                <para PARENT="Default Paragraph Style" ALIGN="1" LINESP="10"/>
                <para PARENT="Default Paragraph Style" LINESP="10"/>
                <ITEXT CH="    Recently, I have noticed an uptick in discussion of the upcoming “Grand Theft Auto VI” video game, particularly in a humorous manner. Every time I visit social media platforms on the Internet, users make jokes about the various goods and services released onto the consumer market (or notable occurrences) before the release of Grand Theft Auto VI. This is usually stated quite casually. For example, one may say “we really having WW3 before GTA6” or “we got aliens before GTA6.” I do not understand the value of these so-called jokes. Simply stating that one event happened before another is not funny, not to mention the egregious grammatical errors present in most examples of the meme format. Nobody would laugh if I left a comment online composed of the phrase “we really having 3 pm before 4 pm.” "/>
                <para/>
                <ITEXT CH="    This brings me to my proposal. My heart is in the work, and I trust that yours is too. (I hope you understand my joke; it’s quite clever of me to reference Carnegie Mellon University so subtly.) As a CMU student, one concept that I spend ample time thinking about is my Quality Point Average, or QPA. This is much more relevant to our lives than the sixth installment in the Grand Theft Auto franchise. Also, as incredibly intelligent students on par with or smarter than those at MIT and Stanford, we know how to use correct English grammar. So, let us reflect these facts with our “meme culture.” Rather than saying “we got Radiohead touring again before GTA6,” you can say jokes like “CMU commissioned the construction of the Jared L. Cohon University Center before QPA 6.” You see, it is still merely stating a true fact, but in a more sophisticated fashion. Not only does it bring our minds back to the university we all attend, but as the brighter among you may recognize, one cannot possess a QPA of 6, making it incredibly humorous. From now on, I urge you: if you see a comment about Grand Theft Auto VI written in this manner, remind the author of their place in this world. Reply to “ratio” them with an even funnier Quality Point Average joke, exerting your dominance as an intellectual student of Carnegie Mellon University. "/>
                <para/>
                <para/>
            </StoryText>
        </PAGEOBJECT>
        <PAGEOBJECT XPOS="849.6242" YPOS="1115.1843" OwnPage="-1" ItemID="239336592" PTYPE="2" WIDTH="232.0625" HEIGHT="397.062100000001" FRTYPE="0" CLIPEDIT="0" PWIDTH="1" PLINEART="1" LOCALSCX="0.24" LOCALSCY="0.24" LOCALX="-72" LOCALY="0" LOCALROT="0" PICART="1" SCALETYPE="1" RATIO="1" Pagenumber="0" PFILE="5.5 images/How to Deal with Those Fuckass Scooters.png" IRENDER="0" EMBEDDED="0" path="M0 0 L232.062 0 L232.062 397.062 L0 397.062 L0 0 Z" copath="M0 0 L232.062 0 L232.062 397.062 L0 397.062 L0 0 Z" gXpos="849.6242" gYpos="1947.1875" gWidth="0" gHeight="0" LAYER="0" NEXTITEM="-1" BACKITEM="-1"/>
        <PAGEOBJECT XPOS="411.7508" YPOS="148.1612" OwnPage="0" ItemID="239345088" PTYPE="4" WIDTH="288.4359" HEIGHT="423.2888" FRTYPE="0" CLIPEDIT="1" PWIDTH="1" PLINEART="1" LOCALSCX="1" LOCALSCY="1" LOCALX="0" LOCALY="0" LOCALROT="0" PICART="1" SCALETYPE="1" RATIO="1" COLUMNS="2" COLGAP="7.2" AUTOTEXT="0" EXTRA="0" TEXTRA="0" BEXTRA="0" REXTRA="0" VAlign="0" FLOP="1" PLTSHOW="0" BASEOF="0" textPathType="0" textPathFlipped="0" path="M0 0 L288.436 0 L288.436 423.289 L0 423.289 L0 0 Z" copath="M0 0 L288.435 0 L288.435 423.289 L0 423.289 L0 0 Z" gXpos="411.7508" gYpos="148.1612" gWidth="268.411518861382" gHeight="214.4468" LAYER="0" NEXTITEM="-1" BACKITEM="-1">
            <StoryText>
                <DefaultStyle LINESP="10" FONT="Bell MT Regular" FONTSIZE="9" KERN="0"/>
                <ITEXT FONT="Bell MT Bold" FONTSIZE="9" CH="Bertie Wooster"/>
                <para PARENT="Default Paragraph Style" ALIGN="1" LINESP="10"/>
                <ITEXT FONT="Bell MT Italic" FONTSIZE="9" CH="387.44 Million Miles of Hate"/>
                <para PARENT="Default Paragraph Style" ALIGN="1" LINESP="10"/>
                <para PARENT="Default Paragraph Style" ALIGN="0" LINESP="10"/>
                <ITEXT CH="    “Alright everyone, if we could gather in a semicircle—yes, perfect—watch your step there. Welcome to Carnegie Mellon University! My name is Victor and I’ll be your tour guide today."/>
                <para/>
                <ITEXT CH="    Behind me you’ll see one of our most iconic landmarks: Walking to the Sky. Feel free to take a photo, just no flash. Sudden stimuli can … interfere with the finish.”"/>
                <para/>
                <ITEXT CH="    Everyone squints upwards. "/>
                <para/>
                <ITEXT CH="    “Yes, it is quite tall. The sculpture represents aspiration, forward motion, the limitless potential of CMYou.”"/>
                <para/>
                <ITEXT CH="    A faint, papery crack as one of the figures shifts almost imperceptibly."/>
                <para/>
                <ITEXT CH="    “That lifelike texture is intentional,” Victor continues lightly. “The artist wanted to evoke the immediacy of the human form.”"/>
                <para/>
                <ITEXT CH="    Another pause. A student at the back raises a hand. “Did—did that one just blink?”"/>
                <para/>
                <ITEXT CH="    Victor smiles. “Great question! CMU is interdisciplinary. What you’re seeing is the result of a collaboration between the School of Art and the Robotics Institute.”"/>
                <para/>
                <ITEXT CH="    The wind carries something down from above. A sound, thin and strained, almost a word. "/>
                <para/>
                <ITEXT CH="     A mother tilts her head. “It sounded like—”"/>
                <para/>
                <ITEXT CH="    “—wind through structural elements,” says Victor smoothly. “Totally normal. Each figure is selected to remind them of their commitment to campus life.”"/>
                <para/>
                <ITEXT CH="    The lowest figure’s jaw trembles. The paint at the corners of its mouth has cracked, revealing something damp beneath the lacquer."/>
                <para/>
                <ITEXT CH="    Victor steps slightly to his left, blocking the view. “Fun fact: our retention rate is among the highest in the nation. This is because some students become such a vital part of the Carnegie Mellon community they’re invited to join it permanently.”"/>
                <para/>
                <ITEXT CH="    Another eerie noise, this time a soft moaning, reminiscent of a sob."/>
                <para/>
                <ITEXT CH="    “We do ask that all participants refrain from vocalization during tour hours,” Victor announces, louder now. “This ensures we don’t disrupt visiting families or negatively affect yield.”"/>
                <para/>
                <ITEXT CH="    A drop of something dark hits the pavement. Victor’s sneaker slides over it. “Just preservative. They’re selected posthumously, of course. Lab accidents, shuttle crashes, food poisoning. We honor our fallen with our Robotics Institute’s pioneering neural reanimation.”"/>
                <para/>
                <ITEXT CH="    A student lowers his phone. “So they’re zom—”"/>
                <para/>
                <ITEXT CH="    “Yes, they’re legacy students.” This joke is greeted by faces almost as lifeless as those above."/>
                <para/>
                <ITEXT CH="    “Isn’t it inspiring,” he continues, “how still they are? That level of professional polish is something you’ll develop here.”"/>
                <para/>
                <ITEXT CH="    “Anyways, we’ll be heading to the Cohon University Center next. And remember: at Carnegie Mellon University, our heart is in the work.”"/>
                <para/>
                <para/>
                <para/>
                <para/>
                <para/>
            </StoryText>
        </PAGEOBJECT>
